> Can you summarize the following section from a report, into a few shorter bullet point "key messages" for Ministries of Finance?
> ===
> This review demonstrates the importance of economic and financial decision-makers - including in Finance Ministries - incorporating climate risks and opportunities to invest in adaptation within their macroeconomic and budget projections and capital investment planning processes. This will be essential to help countries plan for the rising fiscal demands from climate change and invest in adaptation measures to offset the risks. 
> To do this, there is a need for Finance Ministries and other economic decision-makers to start by investing in building their analytical capabilities to identify and assess risks and opportunities. 
> Finance Ministries - working with other departments across government - have a number of ways in which they can do this. Options include: 
> •	Assessing risks and impacts. As outlined throughout this report, there are a growing number of studies, tools and models that can help MoFs understand the current and potential future macroeconomic and fiscal impacts of climate change for their economies. Many of these approaches are captured in a recent report of the Coalition of Finance Ministers for Climate Action on How Ministries of Finance Can Assess and Manage Physical Climate Risks and Adaptation: Available Analytical Tools and Emerging Good Practice (Coalition of Finance Ministers for Climate Action, 2025a). Approaches include a diverse portfolio of models and other approaches including econometric modelling, IAMs, CGEs, catastrophe modelling, scenario-based approaches, and many others. Finance Ministries should take into account the strengths and limitations of different approaches, and look to go beyond traditional approaches to include factors such as tipping points and non-marginal change. Assessments can start with systematically identifying potential climate hazards and quantifying their economic impacts across multiple time horizons using a range of tools. 
> •	Developing a long-term vision for what a well-adapted economy might look like. Once they better understand the risks, Ministries of Finance can work with other departments across government to set clear goals for the level of climate change an economy must be prepared to withstand and set out a vision for what a “well-adapted” country could look like.  This should start with a more in-depth analysis of the current risks and existing adaptation costs and benefits (macroeconomic, fiscal and the effects on the public finances, and economic), and then look to the future, taking account of uncertainty.
> •	Appraising adaptation  strategies and creating the enabling conditions: In the context of delivering on this overall vision, Finance Ministries can support the strategic integration of  adaptation into national development planning and expenditure frameworks, and assess the potential implications for the economy and the public finances. They can also support the enabling conditions for adaptation, including through policy reforms or broader public financial management strategies. Many relevant assessment approaches are also captured in a recent report of the Coalition of Finance Ministers for Climate Action (Ibid). Given that the future macroeconomic and fiscal risks of climate change involve deep uncertainty, actors can use an adaptive management approach to identify early urgent priorities for investment. One approach is laid out in Watkiss and Betts (2021) which includes: first, prioritising early no regret adaptation options that generate early economic and fiscal returns (high benefit-cost ratios); second, ensuring adaptation is built into all near-term infrastructure decisions with long lifetimes: and third, investing in information evidence to improve future decisions. 
> More broadly, Finance Ministries can use the type of analytical exercises outlined above to play a role with others across government in developing national adaptation plans, including by ensuring it is well aligned with national budget processes. They may want to consider including contingent liabilities from physical risks in the fiscal planning and budget process; introducing climate and nature stress-testing requirements for financial institutions; working with central banks to better understand and manage sources of vulnerability for the financial system: and consider how to build natural disaster clauses into debt management strategies with creditors.
